⍝ 04 · Prime numbers up to 100, two ways
⍝ Run:  dyalog sample-scripts/04-primes.apls

n←100

⍝ 1. Count divisors: a number is prime when it has exactly 2.
⍝    (⍳n)∘.|⍳n is a table of remainders; 0= marks divisors.
p←(2=+⌿0=(⍳n)∘.|⍳n)/⍳n
⎕←'primes ≤ ',(⍕n),':' ⋄ ⎕←p
⎕←'how many: ',⍕≢p

⍝ 2. Sieve of Eratosthenes, as a recursive dfn.
Sieve←{0=≢⍵:⍬ ⋄ (⊃⍵),∇(0≠(⊃⍵)|⍵)/⍵}
⎕←'same with a sieve? ',⍕p≡Sieve 1↓⍳n

⍝ Twin primes: neighbours that differ by 2. (2-/p is p[i]-p[i+1], so ¯2.)
⎕←'twin primes:' ⋄ ⎕←(¯2=2-/p)/2,/p
